-- 1. travel-to-ella-with
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Getting to Ella isn''t complicated, but doing it right makes all the difference. Here''s what you need to know about private drivers, the train option, and when each makes sense.',
    content = '<p>Most people reach Ella one of two ways: the Kandy–Ella train or a private driver. The train gets all the hype, and the first hour is genuinely spectacular — misty hills, tea plantations, that iconic bridge shot you''ve seen on Instagram. After that, it''s a slow, crowded ride where you''re sharing bench seats and fighting for a window spot. Worth doing once. But it eats your whole day.</p><p>A private driver from Kandy or Colombo costs roughly $60–80 for the day depending on your route. You''ll arrive by lunch, you can stop at Ramboda Falls or a tea factory along the way, and you won''t be jostling for elbow room. The downsides? You miss that train experience — which for some people is the whole point. Take the driver if your time is tight or you''re traveling with luggage. Take the train if the journey itself is part of your trip.</p><p>Once you''re in Ella, the main drag is short and walkable. Little Adam''s Peak is an easy sunset hike (30 minutes, no special fitness needed). Nine Arches Bridge is best early morning before the crowds, and yes, it looks exactly like the photos. Ella Rock is the serious hike — allow 3 hours round trip and bring water.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'travel-to-ella-with'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 2. sri-lanka-travel-guide
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Your first trip to Sri Lanka raises a hundred questions. Visa, cash, seasons, what to pack — here''s the kind of advice you''d get from a friend who''s just come back.',
    content = '<p>Sri Lanka is small enough that you can cover a lot in two weeks but dense enough that you won''t feel rushed. The island is roughly the size of Ireland but packs beaches, mountains, wildlife, and ancient cities into one trip. Your dollars stretch — expect to spend $50–80 a day on the ground for a comfortable mid-range trip including a driver, accommodation, and meals.</p><p>Visa is straightforward: get the ETA online before you fly ($35–50 depending on your country). Land at Colombo (CMB) — that''s your only international entry point unless you come via a connecting flight. Currency is the Sri Lankan rupee, cash is still king in rural areas, and ATMs work fine in cities. You''ll want a mix of cash and card.</p><p>Weather is the trickiest part. Sri Lanka has two monsoon seasons hitting opposite coasts. December–March is dry on the west and south coasts (Negombo to Galle). May–October is the east coast''s turn (Trincomalee, Arugam Bay, Nilaveli). The hill country (Kandy, Nuwara Eliya, Ella) gets rain year-round but the worst months are October–December. Plan your route around the season, not the other way around.</p><p>Getting around is easiest with a private driver. A car and driver costs $50–80 per day plus fuel. Driving yourself is doable but chaotic — local driving habits take getting used to. Buses and trains are cheap if you''ve got time and patience. More on drivers in another post, but for a first trip, the driver is the single best upgrade you can buy.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-travel-guide'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 3. sri-lanka-travel-map
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka looks tiny on a world map, but you won''t cross it in an hour. Here''s what you need to know about distances, regions, and what goes where.',
    content = '<p>Sri Lanka is tear-drop shaped and about 430km from top to bottom — roughly the distance from London to Edinburgh or LA to San Francisco. The trick is that the roads are slow. That 430km can take 6–8 hours because the highways stop about halfway and the rest is two-lane roads shared with tuk-tuks, buses, and cows.</p><p>The island breaks into a few main regions. The Cultural Triangle sits in the north-central area: Kandy, Sigiriya, Dambulla, Anuradhapura. This is the ancient capitals zone, 2–4 hours from Colombo. The Hill Country runs south and east of Kandy: Nuwara Eliya, Ella, Haputale. Cool air, tea, hiking. The south and west coasts run from Negombo down past Galle to Tangalle — your classic beach resorts, surf towns, and the fort. The east coast is Trincomalee, Nilaveli, Arugam Bay, and Passikudah — wilder, quieter, seasonal.</p><p>The classic mistake is trying to hit all four regions in one trip. You can''t. The driving alone eats your time. Pick two, maybe three. The math is simple: every move costs half a day. If you''ve got 10 days and make 4 moves, that''s 2 full days in a car. Plan accordingly.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-travel-map'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 4. sri-lanka-travel-tips
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Not the usual "pack sunscreen" advice. Five things that actually matter once you''re on the ground in Sri Lanka.',
    content = '<p>Don''t overplan the food. Sri Lanka has fantastic rice and curry, kottu roti, hoppers, and fresh seafood — but eating well doesn''t mean you need a reservation book. The best meals I''ve had were at roadside places with plastic chairs and a glass counter. If the place is full of locals at 1pm, walk in. If the menu has photos of everything and an English name that sounds like a chain, keep walking.</p><p>The train bookings game changed. The scenic Kandy–Ella train used to be a scramble, but you can now book reserved observation car seats online through the Sri Lanka Railways site. Do this at least two weeks ahead during peak season. The unreserved carriages are chaos — fun for 20 minutes, exhausting for 7 hours. Book ahead or don''t bother with the long haul.</p><p>Your driver is not your tour guide. Private drivers are phenomenal for getting you from A to B, and many have solid English and good restaurant recommendations. But they''re drivers, not licensed guides. If you want deep history at Sigiriya or Anuradhapura, hire a separate site guide. They cost $10–20 and they''ll spot details you''d walk right past. Your driver will happily wait while you tour.</p><p>ATMs dispense large notes — break them early. The machines spit out 5,000 rupee notes (about $16), and street vendors, tuk-tuks, and small shops can''t break them. Buy something small at a supermarket or a slightly nicer restaurant to get change. Hoarding small bills is a survival skill in rural Sri Lanka.</p><p>The dress code for temples is real and enforced. Cover shoulders and knees. Take your shoes off. Women don''t need to cover their heads at most temples, but men should remove hats. The Temple of the Tooth in Kandy is strict — no shorts, no sleeveless, no shoes inside the inner sanctum. They''ll turn you away at the gate. Bring a sarong in your day bag, it works for everything.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-travel-tips'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 5. 10-unique-facts-about-sri-lanka-travel-guide
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka has more leopards per square mile than anywhere else. Also the oldest tree planted by a human. Here are ten facts that actually change how you travel.',
    content = '<p>1. Sri Lanka has the world''s highest density of leopards. Yala National Park has about 25 leopards per 100 square kilometers — that''s more than anywhere else on earth. You''ve got a real shot at seeing one. 2. The island has two monsoon seasons hitting opposite coasts, meaning there''s always somewhere dry. This isn''t a "rainy season" country — it''s a "wrong coast" country. 3. Sigiriya rock fortress has the oldest surviving landscape garden on the planet, dating back to the 5th century. The water gardens at the base are older than anything in Europe. 4. You can see whales from the south coast. Mirissa is the hub — blue whales, sperm whales, and dolphins pass through the deep water just offshore between November and April.</p><p>5. The Kandy–Ella train crosses 46 tunnels and dozens of bridges. The most photographed section is between Nawalapitiya and Hatton, not the famous Nine Arches Bridge near Ella. 6. Sri Lanka was the first country in the world to have a female prime minister. Sirimavo Bandaranaike took office in 1960. 7. Ceylon tea is still auctioned in Colombo''s Tea Auction — the world''s largest tea auction center — every Tuesday. You can visit the Ceylon Tea Museum in Kandy to understand why this industry shaped the whole country.</p><p>8. The Sri Lankan elephant is a distinct subspecies, smaller than Indian elephants. You''ll see plenty at Minneriya and Udawalawe. 9. Anuradhapura has been continuously inhabited for over 2,500 years. The sacred Bodhi Tree there was grown from a cutting of the tree under which Buddha attained enlightenment. 10. Sri Lanka has the highest literacy rate in South Asia (over 92%), which is why almost everyone you meet speaks some English. You''ll rarely need a phrasebook.</p>'
WHERE slug = '10-unique-facts-about-sri-lanka-travel-guide'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 6. best-sri-lanka-itinerary-length
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Seven days, ten days, two weeks. Which one suits your trip? Here''s what you can realistically cover without spending half your holiday in a car.',
    content = '<p>A week is tight. You can do Colombo, Kandy, the Cultural Triangle, and one beach town — that''s it. You''ll skip the hill country entirely unless you fly into Colombo and out via a different route, which adds cost. Seven days works best for a focused trip: two nights in Kandy/Sigiriya, two nights in Galle or Mirissa for the coast, one night near the airport. You''ll wish you had more time, but you won''t feel like all you did was drive.</p><p>Ten days is the sweet spot. Add the hill country — Kandy to Nuwara Eliya to Ella — and keep one coast stop. That gives you a proper cultural triangle visit, the train ride, two or three days on the beach, and no single drive over five hours. Most first-timers land on this length and it works.</p><p>Two weeks lets you do the full circuit: cultural triangle, hill country, south coast, and a side trip to Yala or Udawalawe. You could even split the coast and do Galle for a few days plus one east coast stop if you''re traveling in the right season. Two weeks gives you breathing room — you can spend three nights in places instead of one, which makes a massive difference to how the trip feels.</p><p>Anything over two weeks gets you into slow travel territory: fewer moves, deeper dives, maybe a volunteer stint or a weeklong surf camp in Weligama. That''s a different kind of trip, and it''s where Sri Lanka really shines — when you stop rushing between dots on a map.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'best-sri-lanka-itinerary-length'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 7. sri-lanka-private-driver-guide
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'A private driver in Sri Lanka costs less than you think and changes everything about how the trip feels. Here''s the real deal on cost, logistics, and what to expect.',
    content = '<p>The standard setup: you hire a car and driver for the day at $50–80, plus fuel (roughly $15–25 per 100km). You don''t pay for the driver''s meals or accommodation — they sort themselves. Most drivers speak good English, know the roads, and have regular spots they recommend. The good ones double as impromptu guides. The less good ones take you to their cousin''s spice garden. You learn to read the situation fast.</p><p>Flexibility is the real selling point. Want to stop at a random waterfall you spotted from the road? Go for it. Need to change the route because the weather shifted? Done. Your driver handles the chaos — the tuk-tuks, the wandering dogs, the sudden buses — while you watch from air-conditioned peace. I''ve never met a traveler who regretted getting a driver. I''ve met plenty who regretted relying on public transport.</p><p>Book through your hotel or a recommended service, not random TripAdvisor listings. Ask for a driver who speaks English and has worked with tourists before. Verify the car has AC and seatbelts that work. Pay at the end of each day or agree a fixed total upfront. Don''t haggle to the last dollar — a happy driver is worth the extra $5 a day.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-private-driver-guide'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 8. sigiriya-or-pidurangala
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Two rock fortresses, one famous and one less so. Which one should you climb? The answer depends on how you feel about crowds, stairs, and sunrises.',
    content = '<p>Sigiriya is the A-list attraction in Sri Lanka. The climb itself is fairly gentle — a series of staircases winding up through terraced gardens, past the mirror wall and frescoes, to the fortress ruins on top. The views are exactly as good as the photos suggest. The problem? It''s expensive ($30 entry for tourists), crowded by 9am, and you''ll queue on the spiral staircases. The whole thing takes 2–3 hours including the base gardens. Worth doing if you''ve got time and want the full history.</p><p>Pidurangala is Sigiriya''s less polished neighbor. It''s a bigger physical challenge — the final section is scrambling over boulders, not climbing stairs. You''ll need decent shoes and some upper body strength. But the payoff is huge: you get a panoramic view that includes Sigiriya rock itself in the frame. Sunrise from Pidurangala is the photographer''s choice, because the light hits Sigiriya''s face and you''re looking at it, not standing on it. Entry is $5. It''s quieter, sweatier, and in my opinion, more rewarding.</p><p>The straightforward call: do both if you''re fit and have two days in the area. Pidurangala at sunrise, Sigiriya mid-morning. If you can only pick one, Pidurangala wins for the view and the price, Sigiriya wins for the history and the gardens. Your call depends on what you care about more — your Instagram feed or your knees.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sigiriya-or-pidurangala'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 9. yala-vs-udawalawe-safari
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Everyone asks which safari park to pick. Yala promises leopards. Udawalawe promises elephants. The honest answer is less obvious than you think.',
    content = '<p>Yala is Sri Lanka''s most famous national park, and for good reason — it has the highest leopard density on earth. A morning safari almost always produces at least one sighting, and the guides are excellent at spotting cats you''d never see. The downside is the crowds. Yala gets busy. During high season, you''ll have a convoy of jeeps jostling for position every time a leopard is located. The park itself is stunning — dry scrub, rocky outcrops, coastal lagoons — but it never feels wild. It feels like a natural theme park with a queue.</p><p>Udawalawe is Yala''s quieter cousin. The main draw is elephants. You''ll see dozens of them — herds with babies, solo males, elephants bathing in the reservoir. The park is more open, the sightings are more reliable, and there are far fewer jeeps. It''s about an hour from Ella (easier to reach than Yala from that area) and a solid half-day trip. The trade-off: you almost certainly won''t see leopards. Udawalawe has them, but they''re shy and rarely seen on the main safari routes.</p><p>Pick Yala if leopards are a bucket-list animal. You''ll see one, guaranteed within two safari sessions. Pick Udawalawe if you want a safari experience that feels less like a circus. And if you''re an elephant person, Udawalawe is non-negotiable — the elephant transit home nearby is also worth a stop, a rescue and rehab center for orphaned calves.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'yala-vs-udawalawe-safari'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 10. realistic-10-day-sri-lanka-itinerary
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Most 10-day itineraries online are fantasies. Here''s one that actually works — where you spend more time seeing things than sitting in traffic.',
    content = '<p>Day 1–2: Arrive Colombo, drive to Negombo or straight to Kandy (2.5 hours from airport). Stay in Kandy, see the Temple of the Tooth, walk the lake, eat at the Devonia Guesthouse for proper rice and curry. Day 3: Drive to Sigiriya (2.5 hours). Climb Pidurangala in the late afternoon, sleep at a hotel with a view of the rock. Day 4: Sigiriya morning, then drive to Nuwara Eliya (3 hours) via Ramboda. Stop at a tea factory. Day 5: Drive to Ella (2 hours from Nuwara Eliya). Do Little Adam''s Peak for sunset. Day 6: Morning hike to Ella Rock, afternoon train to Kandy? No — don''t. You''ve already come this way. Stay in Ella. Do Nine Arches Bridge early. Day 7: Drive to Udawalawe (1.5 hours) for afternoon safari, then continue to the south coast — Mirissa or Weligama (another 2 hours). Day 8–9: Beach time. Surf or whale watch or just sit and do nothing. Day 10: Drive back to Colombo (3 hours from Galle area) for your flight out.</p><p>This route skips the east coast entirely (wrong season for most people) and avoids the Colombo city stop (you''ll pass through). It gives you three nights in the hill country, two safari options, and solid beach time. The longest drive is 3 hours. You won''t hate yourself by day 7.</p><p>The common mistake is trying to add Anuradhapura, Trincomalee, Arugam Bay, and Jaffna into a 10-day trip. You can''t. You''ll spend half your trip unpacking and repacking your suitcase. This route is honest about what fits.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'realistic-10-day-sri-lanka-itinerary'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 11. sri-lanka-tours-from-uk-winter-sun
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'UK travelers escaping the British winter: Sri Lanka in December–February is your move. Direct flights, 30C heat, and the west coast at its best.',
    content = '<p>December through March is the sweet spot for UK travelers to Sri Lanka. Direct flights from London to Colombo take about 10.5 hours — that''s shorter than Bali or the Maldives, and you land in a country where everyone speaks enough English that you don''t spend the first day figuring things out. British Airways and SriLankan Airlines both fly direct. You can find return flights for £500–700 in January if you book early.</p><p>The best routes for two weeks: start in Negombo or the Galle Face area of Colombo for a night, head to Kandy and the Cultural Triangle for 4–5 days, then down to the south coast (Galle, Mirissa, Weligama) for the rest. The south and west coasts are in peak season — dry, sunny, 30°C. Perfect beach weather while your friends in London are scraping ice off their cars. Hikkaduwa, Unawatuna, and Mirissa are the main beach bases. All have good hotels and restaurants catering to European tastes without being resort prisons.</p><p>The jet lag is manageable — the time difference is only 5.5 hours ahead of the UK. You''ll wake up early, which works perfectly for sunrise hikes and morning safaris. The only UK-specific gotcha: bring sunscreen. The Sri Lankan brands are weak compared to Boots factor 50. You''ll burn before you feel it.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-tours-from-uk-winter-sun'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 12. sri-lanka-tours-from-australia-family-surf-wildlife
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Australians get Sri Lanka in ways other travelers don''t. Surf culture, camping vibes, and the same casual approach to bare feet and outdoors. Here''s the full guide.',
    content = '<p>Australians are spoiled for Asia options, but Sri Lanka punches above its weight for families who surf and want real wildlife. The flight from Perth is 7 hours — barely longer than flying to Sydney. From the east coast expect 11–13 hours depending on the connection. Direct flights from Melbourne and Sydney to Colombo via SriLankan Airlines or Qantas change the game for families.</p><p>For surfers: Weligama is the beginner hub — consistent, gentle waves, board rentals everywhere. Your kids can learn in a morning. Arugam Bay (east coast, June–September) is the advanced point break with a proper Bali-style scene. Hikkaduwa offers intermediate waves with a party vibe. The surf is less crowded than Bali and cheaper across the board. A board rental runs $5–8 a day. A lesson is $15–20. You''ll find Australian expats running several of the surf camps, so the vibe translates.</p><p>Wildlife for kids: Udawalawe is the easiest safari for families — flat terrain, guaranteed elephants, the elephant transit home with its orphaned calves. Yala works for older kids who can handle early morning jeep rides and sitting still. Pinnawala Elephant Orphanage gets mixed reviews (some find it too commercial, others love the close access). Minneriya is seasonal but spectacular July–October when elephants gather around the reservoir. For beach-nature combos, Mirissa offers whale watching and turtle hatcheries in season. Your kids will remember the elephants and the waves long after they''ve forgotten the temple visits.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-tours-from-australia-family-surf-wildlife'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 13. sri-lanka-tours-from-usa-two-weeks
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Two weeks from the US is totally doable, but you need to handle the jet lag and routing differently. Here''s how to make your Sri Lanka trip work from America.',
    content = '<p>The flights are the main hurdle. There''s no direct from the US — you''ll connect through Dubai, Doha, Istanbul, or London. Total journey from the east coast is about 18–20 hours door to door. From the west coast, add a domestic connection first. The silver lining: Emirates, Qatar, and Etihad all fly to Colombo with good connections, and their business-class deals (if you''ve got points) make the journey comfortable. Economy travelers, budget for a good neck pillow and entertainment.</p><p>Jet lag works in your favor if you time it right. Leave the US in the evening, arrive in Colombo early morning local. Stay awake on arrival. Freight your body into local time immediately — no napping. Americans tend to want action faster than European travelers, so a 14-day itinerary works well. Hit the cultural triangle hard in days 2–5 (Kandy, Sigiriya, Dambulla), take the train to the hill country days 5–7 (Nuwara Eliya, Ella), then decompress on the south coast days 8–13. This rhythm matches the jet lag curve — you''re active when energy is high, chilling when the travel fatigue catches up.</p><p>Costs feel cheap after US prices. A nice meal costs $5–10. A decent hotel room $40–80. A private driver for a whole day $60–80. Most Americans I''ve met on the road in Sri Lanka are surprised by how little they''re spending. The shock comes on the flight home — those intra-Asia connections through Doha or Dubai are where your wallet feels it. Book your flights early.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-tours-from-usa-two-weeks'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 14. when-to-choose-sri-lanka-east-coast-beaches
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'The east coast has Sri Lanka''s best beaches — but only part of the year. Here''s when the east coast season runs and whether it''s worth the detour.',
    content = '<p>The east coast season runs May through October. That''s when the monsoon hits the west and south coasts, making the ocean rough and the skies grey in places like Galle and Hikkaduwa. Meanwhile, Trincomalee, Nilaveli, Passikudah, and Arugam Bay get glass-calm water, blue skies, and some of the best beach conditions on the island. The switch is dramatic — it''s like two different countries depending on which side of the island you''re on.</p><p>The east coast beaches are objectively better than the west. Nilaveli is a long, white-sand beach with palm trees and almost no development. Passikudah has a shallow reef shelf that extends 200 meters out — you can wade to your chest without touching the sandy bottom. Arugam Bay is rougher, more surfer-oriented, but its main beach still beats anything in Hikkaduwa for atmosphere. The drawback is infrastructure. Hotels on the east coast are fewer and less polished. Restaurants are basic. It''s quieter because there''s less to do at night.</p><p>If you''re traveling between November and April, skip the east coast — the sea is rough and many hotels close for the season. Save it for a summer trip. If you are on the east coast in season, base yourself in Nilaveli or Passikudah for pure beach time, or Trincomalee town for a mix of beach, history, and restaurants. Arugam Bay is best for surfers willing to trade comfort for waves.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'when-to-choose-sri-lanka-east-coast-beaches'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 15. sri-lanka-with-kids-family-tour-planning
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka with kids: fewer temple stops, more elephants and beaches. Practical advice on pacing, driver selection, and the stops that actually work for families.',
    content = '<p>The golden rule for Sri Lanka with kids: cap drives at three hours. The roads are winding and bumpy. A 5-hour drive that an adult tolerates with a podcast becomes a meltdown factory with children. Plan your route so you''re never doing more than one significant drive per day, and book accommodations with a pool. It''s not luxury — it''s survival. A pool gives you 30 minutes of peace while the kids exhaust themselves.</p><p>Kid-friendly stops that deliver: Pidurangala is too hard for small children, but Sigiriya''s base gardens are perfect for running around. The elephant transit home in Udawalawe is probably the single best animal experience in the country for kids — orphaned baby elephants being fed, washed, and cared for. The Kandy–Nanu Oya train ride (first 2 hours) is enough for the scenic section without overdoing it. Galle Fort is walkable and has an ice cream shop every few blocks. Mirissa has turtle hatcheries in season and gentle surf.</p><p>Hotels worth knowing: most Sri Lankan hotels are surprisingly family-friendly. The mid-range places (Jetwing, Cinnamon, Heritance chains) all have pools, kids menus, and extra beds. Boutique places in Ella and Weligama can be less accommodating — check for family rooms before booking. And get a driver with a child seat. Seriously. Sri Lankan traffic is the kind of chaos you don''t want your toddler experiencing from a parent''s lap.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-with-kids-family-tour-planning'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 16. kandy-to-ella-train-worth-it
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Everyone says take the Kandy–Ella train. Few admit the first hour is magical and the last three are a grind. Here''s the honest breakdown.',
    content = '<p>The Kandy–Ella train is the most photographed experience in Sri Lanka, and the first hour delivers everything the hype promises. You leave Kandy station, climb into the hills, cross your first green valley on a colonial-era bridge, and feel like you''re in a movie. The tea plantations start appearing. The air cools. The windows stay wide open and the breeze carries eucalyptus. It''s genuinely beautiful. Then you hit Hatton at the 2-hour mark and the novelty starts wearing off.</p><p>The full journey is 6–8 hours depending on delays. The observation car (book ahead) makes it bearable — padded seats, big windows, less crowding. The unreserved carriages are standing-room only, hot, and loud. Most travelers I''ve talked to agree: the Kandy–Nanu Oya section (about 2–3 hours) is the scenic highlight. At Nanu Oya you''re already in the highlands, and you can bus or drive to Ella from there in under 2 hours. That''s the smart play: take the train for the views, skip the death march.</p><p>The counter-argument: some people love the full journey. They read, they nap, they watch the landscape change from rolling tea to dry forest. If you''re the type who can sit on a slow train for 7 hours and be happy, do it. It''s a legit cultural experience. But if you''re itinerary-conscious and every hour counts, get off at Nanu Oya and call a driver for the rest.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'kandy-to-ella-train-worth-it'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 17. minneriya-elephant-gathering-from-sigiriya
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'The Minneriya elephant gathering is one of Asia''s best wildlife spectacles. Here''s when it happens, how to see it from Sigiriya, and whether it''s worth the early start.',
    content = '<p>The elephant gathering at Minneriya National Park happens July through October, peaking in August and September. During the dry season, elephants from across the region converge on the Minneriya reservoir — sometimes 200–300 at once — to drink, bathe, and graze on the lush grass that grows as the water recedes. It''s not a migration in the traditional sense, but it''s the largest seasonal gathering of Asian elephants on the planet. And it''s less than 30 minutes from Sigiriya.</p><p>You''ll leave just before sunset (the 3:30–4pm safari slot). The afternoon light is golden, the elephants are most active as the heat drops, and the reservoir backdrop turns orange and pink. You''ll see family groups — mothers with calves so small they''re almost hidden under bellies, teenage males testing each other, a dominant bull watching it all from a distance. The jeep drivers know where the herds are, and the park is compact enough that you''ll find them quickly.</p><p>Is it worth it? Yes, if you''re in Sigiriya between July and October. It costs about $30–40 for the jeep and entry combined, takes 3–4 hours, and it outshines Yala for elephant viewing by a wide margin. Outside the gathering season (November–June), Minneriya is still decent but the herds aren''t concentrated. You''re better off with Udawalawe or Kaudulla during those months.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'minneriya-elephant-gathering-from-sigiriya'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 18. sri-lanka-monsoon-which-coast
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka has two monsoons. They hit different coasts at different times. Here''s a plain-English guide to where to go and when — no meteorology degree required.',
    content = '<p>Sri Lanka''s weather is simpler than most guides make it sound. The island gets two monsoons. The Yala monsoon (May–August) hits the west and south coasts. The Maha monsoon (October–January) hits the east and north coasts. The hill country gets rain from both but the heaviest falls come October–December. That''s it. That''s the whole system.</p><p>What this means for your trip: December to March is prime time on the west and south coasts — Negombo, Colombo, Bentota, Hikkaduwa, Galle, Mirissa — you''ll get blue skies and calm seas. This is peak tourist season for a reason. Room prices double, booking three months ahead is sensible, and the beaches are busy but not packed. May through October, the east coast (Trincomalee, Nilaveli, Arugam Bay, Passikudah) takes over as the dry zone. You''ll have long sunny days, warm water, and far fewer tourists. April and November are the transition months — unpredictable, sometimes glorious, sometimes washed out. Personally, I find April underrated. The west coast is hot and the skies can haze over, but it''s quiet and the water is warm.</p><p>The hill country trick: Kandy, Nuwara Eliya, and Ella get rain year-round because of altitude. Don''t plan a hill country trip expecting Mediterranean summer. Expect drizzle, mist, a chance of downpours, and temperatures around 15–25°C. The rain adds to the atmosphere — the tea plantations look better in the mist. Pack a jacket. Don''t let a little rain talk you out of the mountains.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-monsoon-which-coast'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 19. sri-lanka-honeymoon-route-what-to-include
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka is a solid honeymoon pick if you skip the backpacker route and lean into the right hotels and pacing. Here''s the route that actually works for couples.',
    content = '<p>A good Sri Lanka honeymoon is about doing less, not more. Three or four bases, each for 2–3 nights. Start at a boutique property in Kandy or the surrounding hills — there are small hotels tucked into tea plantations that feel like treehouses for adults. The Helga''s Folly in Kandy is famously eccentric and romantic. Then move to the Cultural Triangle: a hotel near Sigiriya with a view of the rock (Aliya Resort, Jetwing Vil Uyana). One day for Sigiriya and Pidurangala, one day for a hot air balloon ride over the Dambulla plains if budget allows.</p><p>The hill country leg is where the romance peaks. Wildflower Cottage or the Grand Hotel in Nuwara Eliya have old-world colonial charm. The train ride from Kandy to Nanu Oya is a shared experience that actually lives up to the photos. After the hills, go straight to the coast — but skip the backpacker spots. Book a hotel in Galle Fort itself (it''s expensive but walking the ramparts at sunset is worth the splurge) or a secluded beach resort in Tangalle or Dickwella. The Kahanda Kanda near Galle and the Cape Weligama are luxury options that feel private and curated.</p><p>What you can skip: Colombo entirely (save it for the last night if you need a city fix). Yala is fine but the jeep convoys aren''t romantic. Ella is packed with backpackers and can feel more like a hostel scene than a honeymoon. Pick your quiet corners and invest in accommodations with private pools and good in-house restaurants. The best honeymoon meal I know is a private rice-and-curry dinner on a verandah overlooking a tea plantation, no one else around, crickets loud enough to cover the conversation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-honeymoon-route-what-to-include'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 20. luxury-sri-lanka-slower-routes
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Luxury in Sri Lanka isn''t about gold taps — it''s about taking your time. Slower routes, fewer stops, better hotels, deeper immersion. Here''s the alternative itinerary.',
    content = '<p>The mistake most travelers make is treating Sri Lanka like a checklist. Three nights here, two nights there, four hotels in ten days. The luxury alternative is simpler: pick three bases and spend 3–4 nights in each. You lose the "I saw everything" claim and gain something more valuable — the sense that you actually lived somewhere for a few days rather than passing through with a suitcase.</p><p>A slower south coast route: base yourself at a property like the Jetwing Lighthouse in Galle or Cape Weligama for a full week. From there you can day-trip to tea country (a long drive, but you''re not switching hotels), explore Galle Fort at your own pace, take a cooking class, do a whale-watching morning. You stop unpacking. You find a coffee shop you like and become a regular for four days. It changes the trip.</p><p>The jungle luxury option is Wild Coast Tented Lodge near Yala — a safari camp with canvas suites, plunge pools, and the park literally on your doorstep. You do one safari per day instead of two, you have a proper lunch, you read by your pool. It costs $400–600 a night but includes everything and replaces two or three middle-tier hotels you''d otherwise book. The math works if you value time over mileage.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'luxury-sri-lanka-slower-routes'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 21. galle-fort-south-coast-route
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Galle Fort is Sri Lanka''s most walkable town, and the south coast around it is worth a week on its own. Here''s how to do it right.',
    content = '<p>Galle Fort is the best-preserved colonial fort in Asia and the only place in Sri Lanka where you''ll feel genuinely happy wandering without a plan. The ramparts form a 3km circuit — start at the main gate, walk clockwise, and you''ll hit the lighthouse, the cricket ground, and half a dozen lookout points over the Indian Ocean. Sunset is the ritual: locals jog the walls, tourists take photos, and everyone watches the sun drop into the sea. It''s a daily event for a reason.</p><p>Inside the fort, the streets are narrow, the buildings are Dutch-colonial with whitewashed walls and terracotta roofs, and the food scene is the best in Sri Lanka outside Colombo. The Sugar Bistro for brunch, the Fort Printers for a fancy dinner, the A Minute by Tuk Tuk for excellent Sri Lankan fusion. Stay inside the fort walls if you can — the Amangalla is the ultimate splurge, but the smaller guesthouses like the Fort Bizarre or the Mango House give you the same atmosphere for a fraction of the price. The fort gets busy with day-trippers between 10am and 4pm. Early morning and after 5pm is when it feels like it belongs to you.</p><p>Beyond Galle, the south coast is a string of beach towns running east. Unawatuna is a 10-minute tuk-tuk ride — a pretty bay with decent swimming and a party strip. Jungle Beach nearby is quieter but requires stairs (lots of them). Further east, Mirissa and Weligama offer better surf and a more relaxed vibe. Tangalle is where the south coast gets truly quiet — long empty beaches, big skies, and hotels that feel lost in time. A week on this coast is the right amount. More if you surf.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'galle-fort-south-coast-route'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 22. sri-lanka-surf-planning-weligama-ahangama-hikkaduwa-arugam-bay
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka has four distinct surf zones, each with its own season and skill level. Here''s how to pick the right one based on when you''re coming and what you can ride.',
    content = '<p>Weligama is the beginner''s dream. The bay creates a long, gentle left-hander that peels slowly over a sandy bottom. You can stand up on your first lesson here with decent odds. The surf schools are everywhere, the board rentals are $5 a day, and the water is bath-warm year-round. The trade-off? It''s crowded with beginners, and on flat days there''s nothing. But for learning, it''s the best wave in Sri Lanka. Ahangama, just east of Weligama, offers a more advanced reef break — shorter, steeper, and faster. Medium skill level only.</p><p>Hikkaduwa is the west coast''s all-rounder. It has beach breaks and reef breaks, works at different tides, and suits intermediate surfers who can read a wave and paddle for themselves. The town is lively — more of a party scene than Weligama. Best season is November–April. The water is clear, the waves are consistent, and the left-hand reef break at "Hikkaduwa Reef" is the local favorite. Beginners will find Weligama easier. Advanced surfers will find Hikkaduwa a bit tame.</p><p>Arugam Bay is the serious surfer''s destination. Main Point is a world-class right-hand point break that holds 3–6 foot swell and offers 300-meter rides on a good day. It''s the kind of wave that draws surfers from Bali and California. The season is June–September — exactly when the west coast is flat and rainy. Arugam Bay itself is a dusty town built around surfing, and it lacks the polish of the south coast. The trade-off is the wave quality. If you''re a capable surfer traveling in the northern summer, this is your spot. Beginners will struggle at Main Point but can learn at Whisky Point, a mellow left further down the coast.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-surf-planning-weligama-ahangama-hikkaduwa-arugam-bay'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 23. sri-lanka-private-tour-costs
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Everyone wants to know what a Sri Lanka trip actually costs. Not the "budget travel" numbers or the luxury prices — the middle ground where most people end up.',
    content = '<p>Let''s talk real numbers. A mid-range private tour (car, driver, 3–4 star hotels, meals, entry fees) runs about $120–160 per person per day for two people traveling together. If you''re solo, add 30–40%. If you''re a family of four, the per-person cost drops to about $90–110. The biggest variable is accommodation — you can pay $30 for a clean guesthouse or $300 for a boutique hotel in the same town. The $30 room will be fine. The $300 room will be memorable. Both get you breakfast.</p><p>What''s included in typical tour packages: a car and English-speaking driver, breakfast at hotels, accommodation, and airport transfers. What''s not included: lunch and dinner (expect $10–20 a day for two), entry fees (Sigiriya alone costs $30), alcohol (beer is $1–2, wine is expensive and limited), and tips ($5–10 per day for the driver). Most tour companies quote "excludes entry fees" in the small print, and those fees add up faster than you think — budget $50–80 per person for the main sights over a 10-day trip.</p><p>Where the money goes: accommodation is roughly 40% of your total, transport 25%, food 15%, entry fees 12%, and miscellaneous 8%. The best savings are in food (eating local instead of hotel restaurants saves 50%) and in negotiating an all-inclusive driver package rather than paying per-day plus fuel. Most drivers prefer a flat daily rate. Agree it upfront. Put it in writing if you want to be certain.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-private-tour-costs'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 24. sri-lanka-food-experiences
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lankan food is the most underrated cuisine in Asia. Rice and curry, kottu, hoppers, and the street food you''ll dream about after you leave.',
    content = '<p>The national meal is rice and curry. You get a mound of rice surrounded by small bowls — dhal (lentil curry), a fish or chicken curry, a vegetable dish, a sambol (the coconut sambol, or pol sambol, is the one you''ll crave later), and papadams. It''s simple, nutritious, and every family makes theirs differently. The best version I''ve had was at a roadside place in the hill country where the woman cooking brought out seven small bowls and didn''t speak a word of English. No menu, no prices, just incredible food. It cost $1.50.</p><p>Kottu roti is the people''s food. A flatbread is chopped up on a hot griddle with vegetables, egg, and your choice of meat, all mixed with a scraping sound that''s the background music of every Sri Lankan street. It''s not elegant. It''s delicious. It''s the thing you eat at midnight after a long drive. Hoppers are the breakfast star — bowl-shaped pancakes made from fermented rice flour, crisp on the edges and soft in the middle. An egg hopper has an egg cracked into the center. A string hopper is vermicelli-like steamed cakes. They all come with curry and sambol on the side.</p><p>Street food worth seeking: isso vade (shrimp fritters), kottu (everywhere), samosas from a cart, and the fruit stalls selling king coconut water (thambili) for 50 cents. Drink it straight from the coconut. Avoid salads and raw vegetables from street stalls unless you''ve built up the stomach for it. Drink bottled or filtered water only. And never underestimate the spice level — Sri Lankan food is hot in a different way than Thai or Indian. It builds. Eat cautiously on day one. Go all in on day three.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-food-experiences'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 25. sri-lanka-birding-nature-routes
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka is one of the best birding destinations in Asia, with 33 endemic species packed into a small island. Here''s the route that delivers the most for your binoculars.',
    content = '<p>With 33 endemic bird species and another 200-plus resident species, Sri Lanka punches far above its weight for birders. The island is compact enough that you can hit four or five distinct habitats in a week, and the endemics are genuinely accessible — you don''t need a 4am hike into deep jungle. A well-planned birding route covers the lowland forests, the wetlands, the hill country, and the dry zone forests, all within a few hours'' drive of each other.</p><p>Start at Sinharaja Rainforest — the best single spot in the country for endemics. Sri Lanka blue magpie, red-faced malkoha, green-billed coucal, and the elusive serendib scops owl all live here. A good guide is almost essential. They''ll spot birds you''d walk past and they''ll identify calls you''d ignore. Hire one at the park entrance. Next, hit Kitulgala for the lowland riverine species and the hill-country endemics on the way up to Nuwara Eliya. Victoria Park in Nuwara Eliya town is a solid spot for the pied thrush and Sri Lanka white-eye without leaving the town limits. Horton Plains (early morning, before the fog lifts) gives you the Sri Lanka whistling-thrush and the dull-blue flycatcher.</p><p>For wetland birds, Bundala National Park near Hambantota is the premier site — flamingos, pelicans, painted storks, and migratory waders December–March. Yala and Udawalawe both offer dry-zone birding alongside the mammal safaris. And if you''re on the east coast, Kokkilai Lagoon near Trincomalee is a lesser-known spot for water birds. Bring a scope. The wetlands birds are often distant, and the detail in a scope beats binoculars for identification.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-birding-nature-routes'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- 26. top-20-countries-visiting-sri-lanka-2026
UPDATE blog_posts
SET excerpt = 'Who goes to Sri Lanka? The 2026 visitor data tells an interesting story — India dominates, Russia climbs, and the US market is growing fast.',
    content = '<p>Sri Lanka''s tourism numbers tell you a lot about who the island really appeals to. India is the number-one source market by a wide margin — roughly 25% of all arrivals. Proximity, direct flights from a dozen Indian cities, shared cultural touchpoints, and a visa-on-arrival deal. Most Indian visitors come for short breaks (3–5 days), hit Colombo and Kandy, and leave. They''re not the backpackers you meet in Ella, but they make up a huge slice of the overall numbers.</p><p>The UK comes second, and has been a steady source market for decades. Colonial ties, direct flights, winter sun demand. UK visitors stay longer (10–14 days) and spread out more. Russia climbed into the top three after 2022 — visa-free access, package tour operators in Colombo, and a preference for beach resorts (Hikkaduwa, Bentota) over cultural tourism. Germany, France, China, Australia, and the US round out the top eight. No huge surprises there.</p><p>The interesting shift is the US market. US arrivals grew 30% between 2024 and 2026, driven by new direct flight marketing via Doha/Dubai connections and growing word-of-mouth among the adventure travel set. Americans aren''t coming for the beaches — they''re coming for the wildlife, the train, the tea country, and the sense that they''ve found something their friends haven''t discovered yet. That''s Sri Lanka''s real appeal in 2026: it''s still off the US radar enough to feel like you got there first.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'top-20-countries-visiting-sri-lanka-2026'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;
